  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 158 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nRidging aloft will translate eastward across the region Wednesday \nahead of a shortwave trough digging across the northern Rockies. \nSouthwest flow aloft will increase between the ridge sliding off to \nthe east and the digging trough to the west. This flow will help \ndraw more sub-tropical moisture back northeastward into the region. \nENS mean PWATS climb to above 1.4\" to the east of Highway 83 by \nWednesday evening. This would be 150 to 200 percent of normal and \nnear the 95th to 99th percentile for this time of year. As the \nshortwave shifts eastward from from the northern Rockies into the \nDakotas Wednesday night, expect a cold front to slowly move southeast \nacross the area. This front should become the focus for at least \nscattered convective development. With such high moisture content, \nlocally heavy rainfall rates will be possible. The position of the \nfrontal boundary will have to be monitored the next several model \nruns, as the heavier rainfall will occur near the boundary. It \nappears that the frontal boundary will stall across the area Thursday \nnight into Friday as a subtle shortwave approaches within the \nsouthwest flow aloft. Low pressure appears to deepen across \nnortheast Colorado as this occurs, with the frontal boundary arching \nnortheastward across the area. This could bring another round of \npotential heavy rainfall Thursday night into Friday. Ridging aloft \nthen attempts to build northward into the area for the weekend, \nwhich would bring drier weather to the area.   \n\nOverall temperatures through the period look to average below \nnormal, especially for the Wednesday through Friday timeframe. Some \nmoderation could occur as the ridging aloft builds into the region \nduring the weekend.",
